    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n`:h BufReadPost` states:\n\n> BufRead or BufReadPost:\n> When starting to edit a new buffer, after reading the file into the buffer, before processing modelines.  See BufWinEnter to do something after processing modelines.\n> Also triggered:\n> - _**When writing an unnamed buffer in a way that the buffer gets a name**_\n> - after successfully recovering a file\n> - for the \"filetypedetect\" group when executing \":filetype detect\"\n> Not triggered:\n> - for the `:read file` command\n> - when the file doesn't exist\n\n\nHowever, it doesn't actually fire when writing an unnamed buffer such that it gets a name,  in at least some conventional cases. I have tried this in multiple versions of both vim and neovim from up to two years back and it was already present in those versions, so it has probably been this way for a while. Might be related to this Vim [issue](https://github.com/vim/vim/issues/11724).\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\nnvim --clean\n:enew (optionally)\n:au BufReadPost * echom \"Heya!\"\n:w some/file/path\n```\n\n`BufReadPost` will not fire, eventhough the :write made the buffer get a name. Curiously filetype detection will still trigger when you write a file this way, but this does not seem to be through `BufReadPost`, `BufNewFile` or `StdinReadPost`.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nWriting an unnamed buffer such that it gets a name should trigger `BufReadPost` autocmds.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.12.6\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nyes, vim 9.2\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS 26.11 (Zokor) x86_64\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty 0.49.2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-kitty\n\n### Installation\n\nsystem package manager",
