            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Ah, I see.\n\nThen I don't think there currently is a general event that fires *just* when a buffer is written in  a way such that it gets a name (there is `BufFilePre` and `BufFilePost` but those don't fire on `:w`). It would still be convenient to have that though to activate things when a buffer first starts \"having\" a file associated with it (like how the filetypedetect group gets a special case in Vim internally for exactly this convenience). Unfortunately you can't use `BufWritePre` for this either, because you can't match exclusively on an empty filename as far as I can tell.\n\nI'm not going to pretend like I know much about nvim's codebase nor how easy or difficult this would be to actually implement, and perhaps there are reasons why this was made as a special case for `filetypedetect` rather than a generic event; so forgive me if asking like this is rude or shortsighted, but... I think it could potentially be an autocmd that's fired from `set_rw_fname` in src/nvim/fileio.c?\n\nOr is this something for upstream vim instead of nvim?",
